## Static analysis

After decompiling the binary with ghidra we get the pseudo code below: 

```
void p(void)

{
  uint unaff_retaddr;
  char local_50 [76];
  
  fflush(stdout);
  gets(local_50);
  if ((unaff_retaddr & 0xb0000000) == 0xb0000000) {
    printf("(%p)\n",unaff_retaddr);
                    // WARNING: Subroutine does not return
    _exit(1);
  }
  puts(local_50);
  strdup(local_50);
  return;
}

void main(void)

{
  p();
  return;
}
```

We have 2 functions. </br>
The `main` that executes the `p` function. </br>
The `p` function, which save the user input inside a buffer and verify that the return address of the function isn't a **stack address** (`0xb0000000 - 0xbfffffff`). </br>
If it is then we enter the exit condition else the buffer is stored in the heap by `strdup`. 
</br> 

## Vulnerability found 

The `gets` function write the user input from stdin inside the buffer. `gets` doesn't verify the length of the user input (buffer overrun), which make it a vulnerable function. <br/>
We can write more bytes in memory than the buffer can save, we can corrupt the memory.

